	keywords = {Electron density; Molecular orbitals calculations; Water; Hermitian Operators; N-particle operator; Unitary transformations; Basis set; Coupling energy; Density operator; Quantum state; Electron density; Unitarily invariant decomposition; Reduced Hamiltonian operators},
	language = {English},
	abstract = {For pt.II see ibid., vol.13, p.777 (1978). A decomposition of an N-particle operator as a sum of N+1 components is defined such that, in the case of a model system employing a finite one-particle basis set, the decomposition is invariant under unitary transformations of the basis set. Applied to a two-particle Hamiltonian, this decomposition gives rise to the distinction between the independent-particle energy and the coupling energy defined in previous papers. Applied to the reduced density operator for a quantum state, the decomposition corresponds to partitioning the density into irreducible components. This partitioning is illustrated by graphs of electron density for the water molecule.}
